      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-01-sample/q14",
      "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:30mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-01-sample/q14-figure.svg\" alt=\"Figure 4: sketch of the curve C, which decreases from the y-axis to a minimum and then increases, with the region S between the curve and the x-axis from x = 1 to x = 3 shaded\"></figure><p>Figure 4 shows a sketch of part of the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> with equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=\\frac{x^2\\ln x}{3}-2x+5,\\qquad x&gt;0\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>The finite region <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S\"></span>, shown shaded in Figure 4, is bounded by the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span>, the line with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=1\"></span>, the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span>-axis and the line with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=3\"></span></p><p>The table below shows corresponding values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> with the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> given to 4 decimal places as appropriate.</p><table class=\"practice-table\"><tr><td><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span></td><td>1</td><td>1.5</td><td>2</td><td>2.5</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span></td><td>3</td><td>2.3041</td><td>1.9242</td><td>1.9089</td><td>2.2958</td></tr></table><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Use the trapezium rule, with all the values of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> in the table, to obtain an estimate for the area of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S\"></span>, giving your answer to 3 decimal places. <span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li><li>Explain how the trapezium rule could be used to obtain a more accurate estimate for the area of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li><li>Show that the exact area of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S\"></span> can be written in the form <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac ab+\\ln c\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"c\"></span> are integers to be found. <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></li></ol><p><em>(In part c, solutions based entirely on graphical or numerical methods are not acceptable.)</em></p>",
